<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="refrigerator-repair/kenmore-not-cooling"
  eyebrow="Kenmore Refrigerator Not Cooling · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="Kenmore Refrigerator Not Cooling? First Find Who Built It"
  subtitle="Kenmore is a Sears badge — the first 3 digits of your model number say whether it's a Whirlpool, LG, Frigidaire, GE, or Samsung underneath. That decides the fix. Here's the table, the safe checks, and how long your food stays safe."
  heroImageTitle="Kenmore Refrigerator Not Cooling Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">There's no one "Kenmore" — the model prefix tells you who built it.</h2>

    <p><strong>Kenmore is Sears' house brand. The refrigerators are made by other manufacturers and badged Kenmore, and the first three digits of your model number tell you who — which decides the parts and the diagnosis when it won't cool. So before anyone guesses, find the model sticker inside the fridge and read the prefix. The causes are the usual short list — dirty condenser coils, a failed evaporator fan ("freezer cold, fridge warm"), a defrost system iced over, the control board, and the serious end, a locked compressor or sealed-system fault — but the exact parts follow the real maker. Cleaning the coils and checking airflow are safe to do yourself; the board and sealed system are a tech job. A no-cool fridge is a same-day call — your food is on a clock. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <div class="prefix-table">
      <div class="prefix-row prefix-head"><span>Model prefix</span><span>Actually built by</span></div>
      <div class="prefix-row"><span>106 · 110 · 596 · 562</span><span>Whirlpool</span></div>
      <div class="prefix-row"><span>253 · 417</span><span>Frigidaire (Electrolux)</span></div>
      <div class="prefix-row"><span>795 · 796</span><span>LG</span></div>
      <div class="prefix-row"><span>363 · 364</span><span>GE</span></div>
      <div class="prefix-row"><span>401 · 402</span><span>Samsung</span></div>
    </div>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Food is on a clock:</strong> per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door closed. Keep the doors shut, move critical items to a cooler with ice if you can, and get a tech out before that window closes. Full food-safety details are in the section below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 PREFIX = FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Why the prefix decides the repair</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Same badge, five different machines underneath.</h2>

    <p>A 795 Kenmore is an LG inside. A 106 is a Whirlpool. A 253 is a Frigidaire. They share a nameplate and nothing else — different compressors, different evaporator fans, different control boards, and different failure patterns. That's why "my Kenmore won't cool" has no single answer until you read the prefix.</p>

    <p>It also decides whether there's a code to read. An LG-built 795/796 can show LG-style behavior; a Samsung-built 401/402 can show Samsung-style codes; a Whirlpool-built 106/110 or a GE-built 363/364 is mostly symptom-diagnosed with few no-cool codes. Once we know who built yours, we diagnose it exactly like that manufacturer's fridge — and if you want the deeper brand story, our pages for <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/lg-not-cooling/">LG</a>, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/whirlpool-not-cooling/">Whirlpool</a>, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/frigidaire-not-cooling/">Frigidaire</a>, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/ge-not-cooling/">GE</a>, and <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/samsung-not-cooling/">Samsung</a> not-cooling cover each one.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Kenmore stops cooling</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Dirty condenser coils (check first)</h3>
      <p>Coils caked in dust and pet hair can't shed the compressor's heat, so the fridge slowly loses cooling. The most common homeowner-fixable cause on any Kenmore, whoever built it — and worse in an LA summer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Evaporator fan — "freezer cold, fridge warm"</h3>
      <p>The evaporator fan blows cold air from the freezer coil up into the fridge. When it fails or frost jams it, the freezer stays cold but the fridge warms. The fan part number depends on the maker — one more reason to read the prefix first.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Defrost system iced over</h3>
      <p>A failed defrost heater, sensor, or control lets frost build on the evaporator until air can't pass and cooling drops. The tell is a wall of frost behind the freezer's back panel — and it's often what jams the evaporator fan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Control board</h3>
      <p>The board that manages compressor cycling, fans, and defrost can fail in ways that mimic several parts. We test it rather than guess, and we match the board to the real manufacturer. Tech diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Compressor / sealed system</h3>
      <p>If the compressor runs but doesn't cool or won't start, the sealed system is the fault — a gauges-and-tech diagnosis, and the higher-ticket end. On LG-built 795/796 units this is the part most tied to the linear-compressor era.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before the food is at risk.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Read your model prefix</strong> off the sticker inside the fridge — it tells the tech who built it and which parts to bring.</li>
      <li><strong>Vacuum the condenser coils</strong> behind the rear panel or under a front kick-plate. This alone fixes a lot of gradual no-cool cases.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the interior vents</strong> so food isn't blocking airflow between compartments.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door seal and temperature setting,</strong> and confirm the setting wasn't bumped warm.</li>
      <li><strong>Give it room and a power-cycle</strong> — pull the fridge off the wall, unplug a minute, restart.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> reading the prefix, coils, vents, seal, temperature, and a power-cycle are safe. Do not open the sealed refrigeration system, test the compressor, or replace the control board — those carry shock and refrigerant risks and are our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 FOOD SAFETY -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Heat-wave food safety — when to call today</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A warm fridge is a clock. Here are the numbers.</h2>

    <p>A refrigerator that's stopped cooling is a race against the clock, and in an LA heat wave that clock runs faster. Per USDA and FDA guidance:</p>

    <ul class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Refrigerator: about 4 hours</strong> of safe food with the door kept closed.</li>
      <li><strong>Freezer: roughly 48 hours full, 24 hours half-full,</strong> if the door stays shut.</li>
      <li><strong>Above 40°F for 2 hours or more → discard it.</strong> That covers meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, and leftovers.</li>
      <li><strong>When in doubt, throw it out</strong> — and never taste food to decide. Frozen food can be safely refrozen only if it still has ice crystals or is at 40°F or below.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>That's why a no-cool Kenmore is a same-day call. Keep the doors closed, move the critical items to a cooler with ice, and get a tech out before the four-hour fridge window closes. We answer phones 24/7 and run same-day across most of the area. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Prefix read, coils clean, still warm? Now it's a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When you've read the prefix, cleaned the coils, and the Kenmore still won't cool, the fault is inside — the evaporator fan, the defrost system, the control board, or the compressor and sealed system. Our techs diagnose your Kenmore as exactly what it is underneath, and carry the common Whirlpool, LG, Frigidaire, GE, and Samsung fan and defrost parts for a one-visit fix where possible.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/not-cooling/">refrigerator not cooling</a> guide (all brands), and if it's the fridge warm while the freezer's fine, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/fridge-not-cooling-freezer-works/">fridge not cooling, freezer works</a>. Full brand coverage: <a href="/brands/kenmore/">Kenmore appliance repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/refrigerator-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Kenmore refrigerator not cooling: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>Kenmore refrigerator not cooling? Call today — food is on a clock.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
